;; The built-in settings. This file is read from the classpath, so in a
;; packaged jar it lives inside the jar: an installation puts its own paths
;; and limits in a file of its own, named by CORPUS_PROBE_CONFIG or the
;; corpus-probe.config system property, and that is merged over this one.
;; See README.md.
{:port        8080
 :registry    "dev/corpus/registry"
 ;; locale for CQP's ExternalSort collation (needs gawk on PATH) and for the
 ;; app's own collation of metadata and frequency values, so the two agree;
 ;; all KU corpora are Danish, so a single locale suffices for now.
 :sort-locale "da_DK.UTF-8"
 ;; where CQP saves query results, so that paging or re-sorting a search
 ;; does not run it again; drop the setting to run without a cache and
 ;; query afresh every time. A result nobody reads for :cache-ttl-ms is
 ;; deleted, as are the leftovers of a cqp that died mid-save.
 ;; A saved result holds the whole result, not the page: 12 bytes per
 ;; match when sorted and 8 when not, so a whole-corpus result of the
 ;; largest KU corpus (MEMO_ALL, 64.6M tokens) is 739 MiB sorted and 493
 ;; unsorted, and each of the five sort modes is a separate file. The
 ;; budget must therefore be a good multiple of the largest single
 ;; result: below it, results from that corpus can never be kept at all.
 ;; The least recently read are deleted to stay under it, and reaping
 ;; runs after a save and after a cache hit, so an idle server never
 ;; shrinks: this is a high-water mark, not an average.
 :cache-dir       "dev/cache"
 :cache-ttl-ms    1200000
 :cache-max-bytes 10737418240
 ;; how many cqp processes one request runs at once
 :parallelism 8
 ;; A cqp process is killed after :timeout-ms, which covers reading a page
 ;; out of a result already saved: milliseconds. Running the query is a
 ;; different order of magnitude and gets :query-timeout-ms instead.
 ;; Counting is running the query too, so it gets the same one: giving it
 ;; less would let a corpus time out while being counted and succeed
 ;; while being shown, and a count that fails is left out of the total,
 ;; costing the search pages of hits it has.
 ;;
 ;; Measured on a 64.6M token corpus (the size of the largest at KU):
 ;; counting all of it 2.4 s, a page in corpus order 2.3 s and 0.2 s once
 ;; saved. Sorting all of it is another matter: the locale-aware sorts go
 ;; through CQP's ExternalSort, and sorting every match took 650 s by
 ;; word and over 13 minutes by left context, at 3 to 4 GB of memory and
 ;; a 1 to 2 GB temp file in /tmp.
 ;;
 ;; Five minutes therefore leaves roughly ten times the headroom a real
 ;; search needs (two million matches sort in 3 s by word and 19 s by
 ;; left) while refusing to sort a whole large corpus at all. Corpus
 ;; order and random sorting are unaffected, being internal to CQP.
 ;; Refusing is not free: the sort is killed after five minutes of work,
 ;; and CQP leaves its temp file behind when killed, so a reader who
 ;; keeps retrying a query like that fills /tmp. Sizing /tmp, and giving
 ;; the service a private one, belongs with the deployment.
 ;;
 ;; A sort is paid once: later pages are read from the saved result.
 ;;
 ;; :search-budget-ms caps the whole search, and no single batch may
 ;; outlive it, so this is also the longest a request can take. A reverse
 ;; proxy in front of the app needs a read timeout above it (nginx
 ;; proxy_read_timeout and Apache ProxyTimeout both default to 60 s).
 :timeout-ms       60000
 :query-timeout-ms 300000
 :search-budget-ms 300000
 ;; the corpus index grouping: folders may nest and name corpora by their
 ;; uppercase CQP ID; corpora not named anywhere are listed ungrouped, so
 ;; this tree only ever adds structure, never hides a corpus. PROBE is left
 ;; out deliberately to exercise the ungrouped tail.
 :folders     [{:label   "Litteratur"
                :folders [{:label   "Folkeviser"
                           :corpora ["VISER"]}]}
               {:label   "Folketinget"
                :corpora ["TALER"]}]}
